<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  This service holds the smallest privilege that can still count a swipe, and it
  is written this way for the payment apps.

  UPI and banking apps are required to warn about accessibility services,
  because reading another app's screen is how credential-stealing malware works.
  The two things such a check looks at are which apps a service can watch, and
  whether it can read the screen at all. This service gives the least alarming
  answer to both.

  packageNames is restricted to Instagram and YouTube, so the system delivers it
  events from those two apps and nothing else. At runtime the service narrows
  it further to the apps the user has chosen to count, so with Shorts switched
  off it never hears from YouTube at all. A payment app, a password field, any
  other window are not seen — not by policy in code, but because Android never
  hands them over.

  canRetrieveWindowContent is deliberately OFF. The service does not read the
  screen: it counts on the scroll event's own report of a one-item pager
  advancing (fromIndex == toIndex), which is carried on the event itself and
  needs no window content. Declaring no content capability is what tells a
  banking check this is not a screen reader. Without view ids, the one-item
  pagers that are not the player (Instagram's main tabs, a YouTube channel's
  tab strip) are told apart by direction instead: they scroll sideways, while
  Reels and Shorts scroll vertically.

  flagReportViewIds is gone with it, since view ids require window content.

  It also means the service no longer sees the foreground move to another app,
  so a sitting is closed by the idle timer rather than the instant the user
  leaves Instagram or YouTube — a few seconds of a stale session, never a wrong count.

  typeWindowContentChanged is not subscribed to either. A device dump measured
  it at 84% of all traffic (336 of 400 events), which flooded the inspector's
  buffer and evicted the actual scrolling. It contributes nothing now that the
  state machine is driven purely by scroll events, and dropping it saves battery.
-->
<accessibility-service xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:packageNames="com.instagram.android,com.google.android.youtube"
    android:accessibilityEventTypes="typeWindowStateChanged|typeViewScrolled"
    android:accessibilityFeedbackType="feedbackGeneric"
    android:accessibilityFlags="flagIncludeNotImportantViews"
    android:canRetrieveWindowContent="false"
    android:description="@string/accessibility_service_description"
    android:notificationTimeout="0" />
